; print --only tests its predicate against the running total, whatever
; name the predicate gives it, so print must compute the running total
; whenever --only is given.
;
; print shows no running total, so to save its cost calc_posts computes
; one for print only when an option can read it.  An --only predicate
; can, so --only turns the running total on by its presence alone:
; looking for the word `total` in its text would miss `O` (the total) and
; `T` (display_total).  Without the running total the predicate would see
; each posting's own amount instead: 'O<0' would also match One's
; Assets:Cash posting ($-20), and 'T>15' would match Two's Assets:Cash
; posting ($30) instead of One's Expenses:B, where the total reaches $20.
;
; The running total is $10, $20 and $0 through One's postings, then $-30
; and $0 through Two's.

2024/01/01 One
    Expenses:A    $10.00
    Expenses:B    $10.00
    Assets:Cash

2024/01/02 Two
    Expenses:C   $-30.00
    Assets:Cash

test print --only 'O<0'
2024/01/02 Two
    Expenses:C                               $-30.00
    Assets:Cash
end test

test print --only 'T>15'
2024/01/01 One
    Expenses:A                                $10.00
    Expenses:B                                $10.00
    Assets:Cash
end test
